Subject: Give every chunk of the block allocator a region of its own, so a chunk free is a revoke
Upstream: wireshark 4.6.8
Upstream-Archive: https://www.wireshark.org/download/src/all-versions/wireshark-4.6.8.tar.xz
Upstream-SHA256: c0f1ccf217bc0d3b51a9c03ea178b0f7df682e475da26a2d21cd4a1bdd9579d0
Build-Variants: native port, Capstone domain; inert in full Wireshark and in the PoisonCap arm (every hunk is guarded by WMEM_PORT_CHUNKS, except four lines that name WMEM_FREE_MIN_LEN, which expands to upstream's own expression without it)
Depends-On: wireshark-4.6.8-0001-wmem-authority-hooks.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

The hooks of 0001 give a block ONE region, so an individual free inside a
live block ends nothing. Here the block is held linear and every chunk is
carved from it as a region under a handle taken before the block's first
split: a chunk free revokes that chunk, and a reset still ends the whole
block with one revoke. Carving takes a chunk's range away from any authority
over the block, and upstream keeps its chunk headers and free-list links in
those ranges, so they move beside the block into records with upstream's
field names. The header bytes stay reserved, so a block's layout is
upstream's; placement diverges from upstream's once a free that upstream
would merge is not merged. Free chunks never rejoin: a join needs a handle
taken before the split that separated them, which for a front-carving
allocator also covers the live chunks between them. So there is no merge on
free and no growth in place. Because nothing rejoins, every chunk that can
serve a request is listed -- the links live in the record, so upstream's
minimum of two pointers no longer applies -- and a shrink never leaves a
remainder too small to list. The classes of the hunks are in the sibling
.classes file.

